# NEL — Bilingual & RTL

Arabic is the default locale and renders RTL. English is served under `/en`.
Bilingual is a **presentation-layer** concern: content carries `_ar` / `_en`
fields; layout is direction-agnostic.

## Non-negotiable

- **Tailwind logical properties only.** `ps-` `pe-` `ms-` `me-` `start-` `end-`
  `text-start` `text-end` `border-s` `border-e`.
  **Never** `pl-` `pr-` `ml-` `mr-` `left-` `right-` `text-left` `text-right`.
  This is lint-enforced. A physical property is a build failure, not a nit.
- `dir` is set once on `<html>` from the active locale. Never patched onto
  individual elements.
- **Latin strings inside Arabic text need explicit bidi isolation.** URLs, test
  abbreviations (`CBC`, `HbA1c`), phone numbers and email addresses inside an
  Arabic paragraph will render wrong without it. This is the single most common
  RTL defect in this project.
- Numerals and clock times are Western digits in both locales unless a signed OD
  says otherwise.
- Directional icons (arrows, chevrons, carousel controls) mirror.
  Logos, numerals, media controls and clock icons do **not**.
- Every user-facing string exists in `ar` and `en`. A missing translation is a
  failing state, never a silent English fallback in the Arabic tree.

## Verification

Every UI change is checked in **both** locales before it is done. Every E2E path
runs twice, `ar` and `en`. A screenshot in one direction is not evidence.
